\p{Field note from the Neuromodulation Conference (NYC, 2026), filed under [[conf-neuromod-nyc-2026]]. A device for \strong{targeted noninvasive deep brain neuromodulation}: an MRI-guided transducer array steers focused ultrasound into deep cingulate targets. Filed under \strong{deep brain imaging inference} for its fMRI-localized targeting, and cross-referenced to \strong{sense stimulation & distinct qualia} because the modulated variable is pain \em{aversiveness} — an affective quale, not just intensity. Maps to the \strong{[U] Focused Ultrasound} class in [[bcf-0051]].}

\image{kubanek-fus-pain.jpg}

\subtree{\title{Targeting}
\taxon{remark}
\p{A dual transducer array couples through both temples; an MRI intensity map confirms the focus. Deep-brain circuits engaged in pain aversiveness were localized by fMRI: \strong{subgenual cingulate cortex} and \strong{dorsal anterior cingulate cortex} (targeted regions, t = 3--6). This is the "imaging inference" half — you cannot steer blind ultrasound into sgACC/dACC without per-subject imaging.}
}

\subtree{\title{Effect on pain}
\taxon{finding}
\p{Sham vs Active, n=20 each. Active produced a significantly larger drop in pain-intensity change (\strong{***}). Across days 1--7 following stimulation, the Active arm held a durable ~\strong{−35 to −45\%} reduction versus Sham (significant at multiple days). Conclusion: direct modulation of deep-brain pain-aversiveness circuits yields a \strong{rapid and durable} reduction in pain intensity — a candidate rapid treatment for treatment-resistant presentations.}
\p{Funding: NIH R00NS100986, RF1NS128569.}
}

\p{Why it matters for us: the noninvasive-deep-brain-targeting counterpart to our aspirational tFUS rig (\code{DEV-USTIM-001}, [[bcf-0056]]) — same modality, but with the imaging-guided targeting and controlled trial our bench version still lacks.}
